{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>That you shall not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanite.</b> He bound him with an oath that he would not take a wife for his son from the daughters of Canaan, the cursed seed, because a woman predisposed to calamity will draw a man toward her side, for so did the mother of all living do, and as is known from the matter of Solomon, peace be upon him, with the foreign women he married — Ammonite, Moabite, Sidonian, Hittite — concerning whom Scripture testified (I Kings 11:4): ״נשיו הטו את לבבו״ — 'his wives turned away his heart.' Therefore it was established in Israel as a custom to read this perashah to a groom on the day of his wedding, to remind the people that he should be careful in his marriages, and that he should not take a wife for the sake of beauty, for Scripture says (Proverbs 31:30): ״שקר החן והבל היופי״ — 'grace is deceitful and beauty is vain'; nor for the sake of wealth, for wealth will make wings for itself, like an eagle it will fly to the heavens; nor for the sake of the prominence of relatives and family members, that he might be helped by them and gain power, for he will stumble and be punished in each and every one of these three intentions. Rather, his intention must be for the sake of Heaven, and he should attach himself to a worthy family, for children follow, in their traits by nature, the family of the mother, just as it is the nature of wine for its taste to follow the vessel in which it stood." "<b>And furthermore,</b> in this perashah there is an admonition for a man to take a wife from the daughters of his family and to cleave to his relatives, for so did Abraham do, as it says: ״כי אל ארצי ואל מולדתי תלך ולקחת אשה לבני ליצחק״ — 'go to my land and to my birthplace, and take a wife for my son, for Yitzhak.' And so did Amram, concerning whom it is written (Exodus 2:1): ״וילך איש מבית לוי ויקח את בת לוי״ — 'a man from the house of Levi went and took the daughter of Levi.' For when creatures mate, each one to its own kind, peace is established and multiplies in the world. And if perhaps he takes a wife below his station, his heart will be haughty against her and he will arrive at disgrace; and if he takes one above his station, she will be haughty over him and all their days will be spent in quarrel and strife. And the Sages, of blessed memory, already drew an analogy of families that cleave to one another, all being matched in equal worth, to grapes of the vine with grapes of the vine — a seemly and acceptable thing. And when one is worthy and the other not, the parable says: grapes of the vine with grapes of the bramble bush — an ugly thing, unacceptable. Therefore it is fitting for a man to take a wife for the sake of Heaven: the good shall cleave to the good, and the wicked cannot cleave except with the wicked, for each follows its own kind and its like, for the good despise the wicked, and therefore strife will come when they are joined; for peace is established only when they are mated, each one to its own kind. And this is what is written (Psalms 125:4): ״הטיבה ה׳ לטובים ולישרים בלבותם״ — 'Do good, YHWH, to the good and to the upright in their hearts.' Scripture calls those who are mated to one another, whose intention is for the sake of Heaven, 'good' and 'upright,' and Scripture declares that when the good cleave to the upright, and those who pervert their ways cleave to the workers of iniquity, then there will be peace upon Israel."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:38.473466180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}